.TH FL-LOCK 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.0" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
fl-lock \- screen locker of the fldesktop desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfl-lock\fR
.br
\fBfl-lock \-\-locker\fR
.br
\fBfl-lock \-\-daemon\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without options, \fBfl-lock\fR asks the running \fBfl-wm\fR(1) to lock the
session (it sends SIGUSR1 to \fB$FLWM_PID\fR); outside an fldesktop session it
runs \fBloginctl lock-session\fR. Super+L, the panel's power menu and an idle
timeout lock the session the same way.
.PP
fl-wm then covers every output and starts \fBfl-lock \-\-locker\fR, the lock
screen: the clock, the user's picture (\fI~/.face\fR or the AccountsService
icon), a password field and an on-screen keyboard. The password is checked
with PAM (service \fBfl-lock\fR). Only after a successful check does the locker
exit with status 0, and only then does fl-wm unlock; if the locker crashes,
fl-wm starts it again and the screen stays covered.
.PP
\fBfl-lock \-\-daemon\fR, started at login, locks the session when logind
asks (\fBloginctl lock-session\fR) and before the system suspends, holding
the suspend back for a moment with a logind delay inhibitor.
.SH FINGERPRINTS
When \fBfprintd\fR(1) runs and the user has enrolled a finger (Settings >
Users > Fingerprints), touching the reader unlocks as well; the password
field stays ready meanwhile. After a few mismatches only the password is
left.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings > Screen Lock, or by hand:
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/wm.ini\fR [lock]
\fBidle_minutes\fR (lock after this many idle minutes, 0 = never; programs
holding an idle inhibitor, such as video players, delay it),
\fBcommand\fR (the locker, default "fl-lock \-\-locker"), \fBcolor\fR (the
cover shown while it starts).
.TP
\fI~/.config/fldesktop/desktop.ini\fR [Lock]
\fBOnSuspend\fR, \fBShowClock\fR, \fBClock24\fR, \fBKeyboard\fR (offer the
on-screen keyboard), \fBBackground\fR (an image; empty = the desktop
wallpaper).
.SH FILES
\fI/etc/pam.d/fl-lock\fR
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfl-wm\fR(1), \fBfl-settings\fR(1), \fBloginctl\fR(1), \fBswaylock\fR(1)
